  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatTri_6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "Before starting the trial, you meet with the research team to review
    the informed consent and ask questions about the study. You ask: \u201CWhat if
    the trial treatment doesn\u2019t work for me?\u201D and \u201CWhat if I experience
    worse side effects?\u201D They explain that your safety and comfort are the top
    priority, and you always have choices if this treatment isn\u2019t right for
    you.\r\n\r\nWhat does this mean for you as a patient participant in the clinical
    trial?\r"
  explanation: Participation in a clinical trial is always voluntary. Patient participants
    can choose to leave a clinical trial at any time, for any reason. If a participant
    experiences difficult side effects or feels the treatment is not right for them,
    the care team will work to find the best next step, which may include returning
    to standard treatment or exploring other options. This flexibility is built into
    the informed consent process to ensure patient safety, comfort, and choice remain
    the top priority.
  correctAnswer: You can leave the trial at any time for any reason
  incorrectAnswer1: Once you sign the informed consent, you are a participant until
    the study ends
  incorrectAnswer2: If you want to leave the trial, you cannot return to your standard
    treatment
  incorrectAnswer3: "You can leave the trial if the research team decides it\u2019s
    safe"
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Trials
